{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.4,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["FCC2"],
      "pins": ["FCC2.48", "FCC2.73"],
      "nets": ["NET0018", "NET0152"],
      "observed": "The two VCAP pins of the FCC MCU (STM32H753VITx, pins 48 and 73) carry the labels NET0018 and NET0152. These same nets run to the UART3_FCC2 board-to-wire connector on the FCC Peripherals sheet, so the MCU's internal regulator supply pins appear to be wired to UART signals. No VCAP decoupling capacitor is drawn on either pin.",
      "expected": "VCAP pins on an STM32H7 should each go to a 2.2 uF low-ESR capacitor to GND, not to a signal net. The UART3 signals should land on USART pins of the MCU, such as the PD8/PD9 pins that are already used by the GPIO connector nets.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "FCC sheet (page 2): the left-side pin labels NET0018 and NET0152 sit on the VCAP 48 and VCAP 73 pins of FCC2 STM32H753VITx"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "FCC Peripherals sheet (page 5): UART3_FCC2 Conn_01x04 carries NET0018 and NET0152 on its signal pins"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "FCC MCU | FCC1 | STM32H753VITx (Cortex-M7, 400 MHz, 2 MB flash, LQFP-100)"}
      ],
      "impact": "UART3 communication to the board-to-wire connector would not reach a USART peripheral, and the VCAP regulator pins would be driven by signal lines, which could disable or destabilise the MCU's core supply.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The most likely deviation is that the FCC MCU's VCAP pins (48 and 73) are labelled with the UART3 nets NET0018 and NET0152 rather than being tied to VCAP decoupling capacitors, though the drawing alone leaves this partly ambiguous. The rest of the power, sensing, I2C and LED networks I checked are consistent with the specification."
}

